Loot, lewt, l3wt, l00t:<P>This will usually be the biggest thing you have to worry about once your group is familiar with they way each of you plays.<P>Alpha has adopted the if it's an upgrade for YOU or YOU will use it you get it.  If this applies to more than one person we usually have them roll for it.  The tricky part comes from other circumstances.  Some people in the guild have alts in EVERY category and this is where we usually run into problems.  We try, as a group, to think who could use the item most, someone who plays a char all the time in the guild, but is not in Alpha or one of Alpha's twinks generally gets preference.  If we can't think of any main outside of the group that can use it we start considering our alts.  If no alts need it, we put it in the loot locker.  That can be sold or loaned to people in the guild.<P>One thing you probably wont run into in Bravo that we do in Alpha, 3 of the chars in Alpha are HIGHER than the regular players main character.  I think that Heick and Ghowen are catching up to their alt selves but Eklah is still laggin behind  <IMG SRC="smile.gif" border="0"><P>Anyway just some other things to consider are times zones.  If it's 7 for Joe it's 8 for anyone on the east coast.  That's kind of a late start unless everyone is prepped for that.  Alpha runs into this alot as well.  We start at 7est and play until 10 or 10:30 est.  That's just 3 hours of play.  If we're not in place before hand it severely cuts into exp time.<P>Be Prepared, if you play EQ you have access to the net.  Get maps strategy guids etc.  This will help your play night go MUCH MUCH smoother.<P>I'd recommend using KING loot.  This ELIMINATES ninja looting of good stuff.  Only one person loots, usually the person with the highest Str.  At the end of the night, they trade stuff to the person with the highest cha to sell, get the cash for that and /split it with the group.<P>I'll also recommend not getting impatient with your playing mates.  It doesn't help anything.  If you feel they are doing something stupid bring it up in a pleasant manner and try to help them understand what it is they might be doing wrong.  At first communication is VERY important, after you've played together for a while it becomes second hand.  As you begin to understand the play styles of your group-mates you'll find that you'll be able to do things a pick up group can not even think about.  Things that you'll wish that pick up group you just joined in DreadLands at the north corner of Karnors could do.  In other words you'll get spoiled.  I've seen Alpha group perform feats that I feel people of Ghall's level would have to muscle thru.  It's really pretty kick ass.<P>--------------------<P> <img src="http://people.mn.mediaone.net/raven13/images/Ghall.gif"> 
